ggml-zendnn: GGML_ZENDNN_ONLY_TYPES, so a result can name one kernel

An A/B gets whatever mixture the scheduler hands over. On qwen2.5-1.5B Q4_K_M
that is 3866 Q4_K ops and 669 Q6_K ops, and a number measured over the mixture
cannot say which kernel produced it. That mattered immediately: the Excavator
generation deficit looked like Q6_K's fault, since Q6_K is this fork's weakest
k-quant -- sixteen reductions per super-block against Q4_K's eight -- and it
sits on the decode path of every layer.

GGML_ZENDNN_ONLY_TYPES=q4_K restricts what supports_op will accept, so the
question can be put directly. It answered it, and not the way expected: with
Q6_K handed back to ggml, generation got WORSE, 4.8 against 5.5, because
ggml's unrepacked Q6_K is slower than this fork's. The deficit is in the Q4_K
decode path itself.

Unset -- which is every production run -- this costs one predictable branch.
The decline is counted as a rejection rather than returned silently, unlike
several older paths in this function that return false without touching the
counters and made "accepted but never executed" look like a mystery for weeks.

See docs/perf/q4k_vs_repacked_ggml_a10.csv.

